{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_iefhrjw62muntv5odt6t7flji3pgrxcj2zbmzja25hp6p34csuwq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01NNarnfkTMoGZZB7HjrNr9x","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'PY'\np=\"ERRORS.md\"\ns=open(p).read()\nold = \"\"\"* Rows 2–6 and 11 are allocation-failure paths. They are triggered\n  deterministically by `fork()`ing a child, lowering `RLIMIT_AS` to the child's\n  current VM size plus a small slack, and shaping the input so that exactly the\n  targeted allocation is the one that exceeds the slack. C and Rust each run in\n  their own freshly `fork()`ed child (identical memory state), and the two\n  children's exit codes are compared.\"\"\"\nnew = \"\"\"* Rows 2–6 and 11 are allocation-failure paths. They are triggered\n  deterministically by `fork()`ing a child, lowering `RLIMIT_AS` to the child's\n  current VM size plus a small slack (8 MiB), and shaping the input so that\n  exactly the targeted allocation is the one that exceeds the slack. C and Rust\n  each run in their own freshly `fork()`ed child (identical memory state), and\n  the two children's exit codes are compared.\n  The targeted allocation is **128 MiB**, deliberately larger than glibc's\n  `DEFAULT_MMAP_THRESHOLD_MAX` (32 MiB on 64-bit): smaller requests can be\n  satisfied out of a thread arena's already-reserved 64 MiB of address space, in\n  which case `RLIMIT_AS` would not stop them and the row would not actually be\n  exercised (this was observed and fixed during Phase C).\"\"\"\nassert old in s\ns=s.replace(old,new,1)\nopen(p,\"w\").write(s)\n\np=\"SYMBOLS.md\"\ns=open(p).read()\nold = \"\"\"The Rust `.so` imports `malloc` / `realloc` (used directly, so the returned\nbuffers are `free()`-able by the caller exactly as in C) plus the usual Rust\nruntime/libc set (`memcpy`, `memmove`, `memset`, `bcmp`, `free`, `calloc`,\n`posix_memalign`, `strlen`, `abort`, `_Unwind_*`, `dl_iterate_phdr`, pthread\nTLS helpers, and file/IO syscalls used by the panic-backtrace machinery).\"\"\"\nnew = \"\"\"The Rust `.so` imports **the same six libc functions** — `malloc`, `realloc`,\n`strdup`, `strlen`, `strncpy`, `strstr` — because the translation calls the very\nsame libc primitives the C calls (guaranteeing identical `strstr`/`strncpy`\ncorner-case behaviour, identical allocator, and identical faulting behaviour on\nNULL input in every build profile). On top of that it imports the usual Rust\nruntime set (`memcpy`, `memmove`, `memset`, `bcmp`, `free`, `calloc`,\n`posix_memalign`, `abort`, `_Unwind_*`, `dl_iterate_phdr`, pthread TLS helpers\nand the file/IO syscalls used by the panic-backtrace machinery).\"\"\"\nassert old in s\ns=s.replace(old,new,1)\nopen(p,\"w\").write(s)\nprint(\"ok\")\nPY","description":"Update notes in ERRORS.md and SYMBOLS.md"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4286,"cache_read_input_tokens":164238,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4286,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8bc34e8f-fde7-4761-8d52-c1baa11cda45","uuid":"5d2c544e-c7db-4dc8-9624-5daeb02d185a","timestamp":"2026-08-20T15:03:50.697Z"}
